---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Find a hearing",
    body: "Committees announce public hearings a few days ahead. Watch the calendar here, or follow a bill to see its hearings, every hearing links to the official notice with the room, time, and agenda.",
    links: [{ href: "/calendar/", label: "Civic calendar" }],
  },
  {
    title: "Read the official notice",
    body: "The notice lists every bill on the agenda in order. Committees usually take bills in agenda order, but chairs can rearrange, arriving early is the only guarantee you'll be there when your bill comes up.",
    links: [],
  },
  {
    title: "Fill out a hearing slip when you arrive",
    body: "Outside the hearing room you'll find appearance slips. Fill one out with your name, who you represent (yourself counts), the bill number, and whether you are for, against, or providing information only. You can register your position without speaking, registrations are read into the record.",
    links: [],
  },
  {
    title: "Testifying",
    body: "The chair calls speakers from the slips. Keep remarks short (chairs often set limits when rooms are full), speak to how the bill affects you, and expect questions from members, it's a conversation, not a speech. Bringing printed copies of longer testimony for the members is welcome.",
    links: [],
  },
  {
    title: "Can't attend? Write instead",
    body: "Committee pages list every member; you can email your testimony to the committee chair and members before the hearing and ask that it be included in the record. And your own legislators want to hear from constituents regardless of committee membership.",
    links: [
      { href: "/committees/", label: "Committees and their members" },
      { href: "/my-reps/", label: "Find your legislators" },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Afterward",
    body: "A public hearing doesn't mean a vote. The committee may later hold an executive session to vote the bill forward, follow the bill here to see every action, or check whether it ever got a hearing at all.",
    links: [
      { href: "/hearing-none/", label: "Bills that died without a hearing" },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="How to Testify"
  description="A practical guide to testifying at a Wisconsin legislative committee hearing: finding hearings, appearance slips, speaking, and written testimony."
>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold">How to Testify at a Hearing</h1>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-3xl text-sm text-navy-700">
    Public hearings are where individual voices carry the most weight in the
    Legislature. Committees hear from anyone who shows up, in the order they
    signed in. Here's how it works, start to finish. For the Legislature's own
    description of the process, see the
    <a href="https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/document/help/process" class="underline">official overview</a>.
  </p>

  <ol class="mt-6 max-w-3xl space-y-4">
    {steps.map((s, i) => (
      <li class="rounded-lg border border-navy-100 bg-white p-4">
        <h2 class="flex items-baseline gap-3 text-lg font-bold">
          <span class="flex h-7 w-7 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-navy-900 text-sm text-white" aria-hidden="true">{i + 1}</span>
          {s.title}
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-navy-700">{s.body}</p>
        {s.links.length > 0 && (
          <p class="mt-1 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 text-sm">
            {s.links.map((l) => (
              <a href={l.href} class="py-1 underline">{l.label}</a>
            ))}
          </p>
        )}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-sm text-navy-600">
    Practical details (rooms, slips, chair discretion) reflect standing
    practice in the Wisconsin State Capitol; the hearing notice for your
    hearing is always the authority on time and place.
  </p>
</Base>
